 26	/**
 27	 * The dispatcher (AC-12 – AC-16).
 28	 *
 29	 * <p>The ordering here is the reliability guarantee, and each step is a separate transaction on
 30	 * purpose:
 31	 *
 32	 * <ol>
 33	 *   <li><b>Claim.</b> One conditional {@code UPDATE} whose {@code WHERE} carries {@code state =
 34	 *       'QUEUED'} and an expired-or-absent lease. Two dispatchers racing one row produce exactly
 35	 *       one {@code 1}. A pass selects and claims in that same statement ({@link
 36	 *       StudioDispatchLedgerStatements#claimQueued}, {@code FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED}), so two
 37	 *       dispatchers draining one backlog take different rows instead of racing for the same ones.
 38	 *   <li><b>Recheck and resolve, immediately before the send and after the claim.</b> Authorization
 39	 *       and reference availability are re-verified and reference values are resolved then and only
 40	 *       then. Values are ephemeral: they live inside a try-with-resources and are erased on every
 41	 *       exit path including the error path, and they are written to no ledger column, no history
 42	 *       row, no log line, no retry buffer, no manifest and no SSE payload.
 43	 *   <li><b>Commit {@code DISPATCHING} and a fresh attempt id, then send.</b> The commit is a
 44	 *       transaction of its own and it closes before the HTTP call begins, so a second connection
 45	 *       can observe the committed row while the runner client is still blocked. An {@code
 46	 *       attempt_id} is never reused across attempts.
 47	 *   <li><b>Classify.</b> Exactly three outcomes and no fourth (AC-15). An ambiguous outcome is not
 48	 *       permission to resend: the row stays {@code DISPATCHING} and recovery resolves it.
 49	 * </ol>
 50	 *
 51	 * <p><b>This dispatcher no longer holds a callback base URL.</b> §8.4 makes the callback
 52	 * destination deployment configuration and <em>"never arbitrary job-supplied URL"</em>; the runner
 53	 * reads its own {@code STUDIO_RUNNER_CALLBACK_BASE_URL} and keeps a {@code CALLBACK_SHAPED_KEYS}
 54	 * list to refuse any job that tries to name one. Sending it was tripping that control from the
 55	 * inside, so the field is gone rather than merely unread.
 56	 *
 57	 * <p>If the process dies between the commit in step 3 and the send, §5.1 explicitly permits the
 58	 * outcome to end as <em>unknown</em> rather than risk duplicate effects. Ending as unknown is the
 59	 * correct answer there; a "fix" that replays it is the failure.
 60	 */
