## 4. Trust boundaries, value lifetime, password-only ruling

| Boundary | Rule |
|---|---|
| Gateway (model) | Receives step text with the `<secret ID>` span masked plus the reference `{kind:secretRef, referenceName:ID}`. The ID is permitted (GA plan §7 AC-1); the value is structurally impossible (no `value` field on the kind). Outgoing-bytes canary test. |
| Backend DB | Stores only IDs, vault **version identifiers** and audit rows (§4.1). The dispatch frame is assembled after `GuidedSessionService.consume()` commits (`GuidedDispatchService.java:80-100`) and is never journaled; `Resolved` is try-with-resources (`StudioReferenceResolver.Resolved.close()`). |
| Controller → runner bridge | The only channels carrying a value: `BridgeDispatch.secret` (guided, on the mTLS + RS256 JWT chain — `GuidedInternalSecurity.java`, `guided/transport.ts:59-61`) and the `/v2/runs` job → `Launch3/Execute3.secrets` (replay, on the backend→runner hop, which becomes TLS with client-certificate authentication of the backend under Q4/G-2; today plaintext `node:http` with a static bearer, `server.ts:17,33`). One runner process serves both listeners' routes (ADR-SC-005 default). Closed key sets; schema-validated before send and on receipt. |
| Runner memory | Value lives in the dispatch frame until `withhold([value])` copies it into the collector's private inventory (`browser.ts:335`), then the frame reference is dropped. Inventory wiped at `dispose()` (`browser.ts:452`) and on `stop`. The runner itself writes no scratch file, argv or env with a value (legacy rule, `protocol.ts:106`); the browser profile is a separate sink, next row. |

| Inv. | Statement, evidence, story |
|---|---|
| **I-A1 Step binding** | A secret is filled only (a) at a step whose authored source declares `<secret ID>`, (b) in slot `fill.value`, (c) into the unique visible password control resolved by trusted runner code in the collector's isolated world, pinned at `prepare` and proven to be the same attached element at fill (§4.2, R1-1/R1-3; the model never sees a password node), (d) with an owning `form.action` origin inside `allowedOrigins` (R1-2) and on an origin in `allowedOrigins ∩ approvedOrigins`. *Guided proposal:* `GuidedProposalValidator.java:323-339` joins obligation → source reference → authored input, whose `sourceStepId` is the declaring step (classifier `:152-171`); runner `guided/runtime.ts:143` insists `input.sourceStepId === p.coordinate.stepId && intendedSlot === slot`; (c)/(d) per §4.2/§4.3. *Artifact/replay:* `inputRecipes[].stepId` is the declaring step; `validator/observedReplay.ts:261` already demands `input.stepId === ob.stepId && intendedSlot === slotOf(kind)` for `authoredLiteral` — extend identically for `secretRef`, and the executor re-checks at `perform`. *Retired:* the artifact-1 binding in `validator/references.ts:101-170` and `requireSecretOrigin` (`playwrightAdapter.ts:504-520`) leave with the compile path (OBS-012A) and are not cited as evidence for the single path. **Story: OBS-007.** |
| **I-A2 Just-in-time delivery and scoping** | *Guided:* the value travels only in the `BridgeDispatch.secret` of the admitted fill, built after `consume()` commits (`GuidedDispatchService.java:80-100`), withheld before the fill, inventory wiped at `dispose()` (`browser.ts:452`). *Observed replay — decision: keep run-scoped delivery.* All declared secrets are resolved once at dispatch and shipped in the `/v2/runs` job body (`StudioDispatcher.java:320-322,501-506`; `protocol.ts:106-113`). Per-step delivery was evaluated and rejected: it would add a second secret-bearing channel (backend → runner per step) and a backend-driven step loop that the autonomous artifact executor does not have, require either holding values in the backend across the run or N Vault reads for one authorization decision, and the value would still sit in child memory from that step to exit. Residency shrinks from minutes to seconds at the cost of a new channel on a hop that is not yet TLS-authenticated (I-C8). Revisit only if Security requires it after I-C8 lands. *Scoping (verified):* never cached across runs — `Resolved` is try-with-resources and erased on every exit path including throw (`StudioReferenceResolver.Resolved.close()`; `VaultStudioReferenceResolver.java:129-135`); the runner parent wipes on success/failure/throw/drain/cancel (`runCoordinator.ts:45,332-357,946`); one forked child per run with an enumerated environment (`launcher.ts:260-264,387-388`) so concurrent runs share no process; the child receives `stop`, then `SIGKILL` of its process tree within `stopTimeoutMs` (default 5 s; `launcher.ts:422,489-494,309-315`). *Guided worker:* `knownSecrets=[]` and inventory dispose on `dispose()`, one browser context per session — but in-process browsers share one heap, so inter-session isolation is by `capacity` (default 1, `ownership.ts:101`); **required (F-10):** the mounted config asserts `capacity === 1` while secret fills are enabled, or the two-session cross-value isolation oracle must pass. **Residual (stated):** JVM `String` copies and JavaScript strings cannot be zeroed (`StudioReferenceResolver.Resolved` javadoc; `runCoordinator.ts:332`); the decidable property is that no live reference survives wipe and the child process exits. On replay the values are resident in the child from the `execute` message until exit. *Read side (F-4):* the Vault response is parsed into a Jackson tree and the value is a `String` before `toCharArray()` (`HashicorpStudioVaultProvider.java:349-360`). *Write side (F-4):* both writers build a whole-body `String` (`HttpRunnerDispatchClient.java:113-116` `writeValueAsString`→`ofString`; `GuidedRunnerClient.java:87-89` `canonical(body)`→`ofString`). **Required:** parse the read with a streaming `JsonParser` (`getTextCharacters`) straight into `char[]`; publish bodies as `byte[]` (`ofByteArray`) zeroed after send. Even then Jackson, `HttpClient` and TLS internal buffers remain a residual. *Disk:* browser profile (§4 table, F-3). **Story: OBS-007.** |
| **I-A3 Authorization at release; no stale frame is ever sent; bounded post-release window** | *No stale frame (verified):* `mayUse` and the Vault read happen inside the dispatch pass immediately before `send` (`StudioDispatcher.java:320-355`); revocation closes `AUTHORIZATION_REVOKED` with no send (`:356-360`); a Vault outage releases the claim and the next pass re-resolves (`:361-367`); a retryable definite refusal returns the row to QUEUED with a fresh `attempt_id` and the next pass re-resolves (`:440-445`); an ambiguous send is never resent (`:447-453`). Observed replay uses the same pass (`:388-392`); v3 moves `references.resolve` inside `ObservedReplayAuthorization.job()`. Guided recovery has three real fault positions: live/fresh same-worker/context/proof `ADMITTED/count0` before consume starts reads0/sends0 and may win consume once, recheck current authorization/origin/evidence, resolve once and send at most once; committed consume/count1 before resolve yields `AlreadyConsumed` with zero reads/sends and no new read/resend; after actual resolve or ambiguous send recovery adds no read/send, erases transient scope, preserves known success/failure and records honest uncertainty for unobserved effects. Context loss cannot rebuild/replay a browser; no `WonConsumption`, value or frame is cached, and no read-and-discard manufactures a counter. `AUTHORIZATION_REVOKED` is backend/API-assigned: own dispatcher denial before send, or terminal runner `ERROR/CANCELLED` mapped only against actual independently recorded `revoked_at`; a runner-supplied cause never assigns it. Actual user cancel retains `ERROR/CANCELLED` precedence when both facts exist, with independent revocation audit. Missing trusted facts/control-network loss stay unmapped cancellation/uncertainty, with no invented cause. Completed `PASSED`, `FAILED`, assertion-failure and `LOGIN_REQUIRED` outcomes are never rewritten by later facts. Keep write-once `revoked_at`, unique `REVOKED_MID_RUN`, worst-phase/slow/lost-response proof and the ≤2 s including request/queue time plus one already-in-flight step residual, with no next-step typing. *Post-release window (F-2, stated honestly):* once released, a value stays usable by the runner until the run ends — replay children hold every declared secret from `execute` until exit, at most `total-runtime` (default 120 s, `application.yml:259`; run deadline 600 s, `:253`); the guided inventory holds it until session `dispose`. Today the control signal carries only cancellation (`ExecutionControlService.java:28-31`; `control-poll-interval: 2s`, `application.yml:297`; `reportClient.ts:69-83`), so a `mayUse` revocation or Vault destroy **mid-run stops nothing**. **Required:** a worker-side revocation sweep rechecks `mayUse` for every in-flight execution/session with a released secret at the control-poll cadence and writes a write-once `revoked_at`; `ExecutionControl` gains `revoked: boolean`, carried on the progress acknowledgement by the same path as `cancelRequested` (`runCoordinator.ts:67,460`); guided sessions receive `stop{reason: REVOKED}`. The run is cancelled within one control poll and an audit row `REVOKED_MID_RUN` is written. **Residual:** a fill already in flight when revocation lands completes; the bound is one poll (2 s) plus one step, not zero. **Required:** `RunJob`/`BridgeDispatch` payloads are built inside the resolution scope and never enter a retry buffer or idempotency store; the backend guided dispatch digest is computed over the request **without** `secret`, and the runner's replay map strips `secret` before hashing — today it hashes the whole body (`guided/transport.ts:72`; F-7). **Story: OBS-007.** |

